  "worked_solution": "The kinetic energy of a particle with mass $m$ and velocity $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{KE}=\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2}.\n$$\n\nWhen the mass and velocity are measured in kilograms and meters per second, respectively, the particle's kinetic energy is measured in Joules ($J$).\n\nFirst, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & =\\int 2t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ v(t) & ={t}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n0={0}^{2}+C\\,⟹\\,C=0.\n$$\n\nHence, the velocity of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)={t}^{2}.$ In particular, its velocity at time $t=2\\,s$ is\n\n$$\nv=v(2)={2}^{2}=4\\,m/s.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kinetic energy of the particle at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{KE} & =\\frac{1}{2}m{v}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot {4}^{2} \\\\  & =16\\,J.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
